 Installation and Compatibility Information
 ==========================================

 What's New in DESQview 2.8

 DESQview 2.8 contains a relatively modest number of changes from
 the previous release, DESQview 2.7.  DESQview's version number has
 changed primarily to reflect a major change in DESQview 386, which
 is that DESQview 386 v2.8 incorporates QEMM 8.0, where DESQview
 v2.7 included QEMM 7.5.  (DESQview 386 consists of a copy of QEMM
 and a copy of DESQview running on the same machine. It doesn't
 matter whether QEMM and DESQview are purchased separately, or
 together in the DESQview 386 retail package.)

 DESQview 2.8 includes a DOS-only version of Manifest, found in the
 DESQview directory (typically C:\DV).  DESQview 386 also includes
 a version of Manifest that presents both a DOS and Windows
 interface; this version can be found in the QEMM directory
 (typically C:\QEMM).

 Other changes to DESQview have been minimal.  In this release,
 DESQview has been enhanced to provide better support for network
 printing under NetWare's VLM, and has been modified to support PCI
 machines that also support the Micro Channel Bus.  An inconsistency
 involving closing programs started via a command-line parameter to
 DESQview has been addressed.  A number of technical notes have been
 added to the DESQview distribution; see the section "Technical
 Notes" below for more details.  Finally, DESQview 2.8 works around
 a problem in DOS's management of the date change at midnight.

 For more information on changes that have been made to DESQview,
 please see the "Revision History" section below.


 DESQview and DOS

 The shipping versions of all Quarterdeck products are compatible
 with DOS versions up to and including DOS 7, the version of DOS
 that comes with Windows 95.  Users of Quarterdeck products should
 observe the following guidelines with our currently shipping
 products and all versions of DOS later than version 4:

 1) DOS's DOS=HIGH option sometimes results in a net loss of
    conventional memory inside DESQview. If you use the DOS=HIGH
    option in your CONFIG.SYS, inspect the DESQview Memory Status
    program with and without DOS=HIGH to determine which
    configuration gives you the largest window size in DESQview.
    Additionally, if you are using QEMM 7 or later, your window size
    will probably benefit from QEMM's DOS-Up feature (see the QEMM
    manual for details).  Again, you should compare the results of
    using DOS-Up with DESQview's Memory Status, and determine which
    configuration is best for you.

 2) Use QEMM as your memory manager if you are running on an 80386,
    486 or Pentium-based machine.  Other memory managers, including
    DOS' EMM386, cannot provide the range of features, especially in
    terms of DESQview support, that QEMM can.

 3) If you load DOS 6's DOSKEY before DESQview, you should load it
    again (with the /REINSTALL parameter) in each DESQview window in
    which you wish to use it. If you do not plan to use DOSKEY
    outside of DESQview, you can save memory by not loading it
    before DESQview.

 4) Do not use DOS 6's DOSSHELL task switcher option with DESQview.


 Microsoft Windows and DOS

 DESQview will run under all versions of DOS including DOS 7, the
 version that accompanies Microsoft Windows 95.  Note that in order
 to take advantage of all of DESQview's features under DOS 7, you
 must not run DESQview in a Windows 95 DOS window.  Instead you must
 boot to a command prompt, or choose the Start / Shutdown / "Restart
 the computer in MS-DOS mode" option in Windows 95.  As with
 previous versions of DOS, your DOS 7 configuration must use QEMM if
 you wish to run multiple large DOS programs, or if you wish
 programs that write directly to the screen to run in background.

 Windows 95 has removed support for standard mode, and thus will not
 run in a DESQview window.  Earlier versions of Windows will run
 under DESQview irrespective of the DOS version.

 DESQview will run in a Windows 95 DOS window. machine in MS-DOS
 mode.  However, in this configuration, Windows 95 has taken control
 of memory management away from QEMM.  Thus in a DOS window that has
 been started under Windows 95, you will not be able to multitask
 large DOS, nor will you be able to virtualize the screen.  This
 means the programs that write directly to the screen will not run
 in background or in a small window under Windows 95.  As noted
 above, restarting the computer in MS-DOS mode, or booting directly
 to the command prompt will allow you to use all of DESQview's
 features if you use QEMM as your memory manager.

 DESQview continues to be compatible with Windows 3.x as described
 in the DESQview manual, regardless of the underlying DOS version.

 DESQview's Learn feature and its Transfer function do not work in a
 DESQview window running Microsoft Windows.  Microsoft Windows takes
 its keystrokes directly from the keyboard hardware, and never sees
 the keystrokes that DESQview's Learn and Transfer place in the
 BIOS's keyboard buffer.

 If you have Microsoft Windows 3.11 or earlier, there will be two
 selections for Windows on the DESQview Open Window menu.  "MS
 Windows 3 Real Mode" runs Windows in real mode and is for Windows
 3.0 only. "MS Windows 3 Std. Mode" runs Windows 3.0 or 3.1 in
 Windows' fastest mode -- Standard Mode.  If you have Windows 3.1
 and you experience problems running Windows under DESQview, use
 DESQview's Change a Program to set the Program field to WIN.COM,
 and put /S in the Parameters field.  Windows for WorkGroups also
 supports Standard Mode, but with the parameter /D:T instead of /S.
 You cannot run Microsoft Windows in 386 Enhanced Mode under
 DESQview.


 Networks

 Normally, you should load your network drivers and log in to the
 network before starting DESQview.  However, it is now possible to
 log in to a file server from within a DESQview window when running
 under Novell Netware or Netware Lite.  Consult Quarterdeck
 Technical Note #194, Logging into NetWare Networks from Multiple
 Windows (LOGIN.TEC). This file may be obtained from the sources
 listed in the section "Contacting Quarterdeck" below.


 Lantastic and Novell Personal Netware Networks

 DESQview now includes complete compatibility with Lantastic and
 Novell Personal Netware (Netware Lite).  If you are running on a
 machine configured as a network workstation, it is important to
 enable IPX virtualization for Netware Lite, and NETBIOS
 virtualization for Lantastic, in DESQview Advanced Setup, under the
 Network option. If you are running DESQview on a peer-to-peer
 server, you should also enable the peer-to-peer option appropriate
 to your network on this screen.


 NODESQ and NOVER

 Many applications have built-in support for DESQview or for TopView
 (an IBM multitasking environment with which DESQview is
 compatible).  DESQview or TopView support can allow an application
 to run better under DESQview, or to be more accommodating to other
 programs running at the same time.  However, as with any aspect of
 software, there may be bugs in an application's DESQview support;
 in this case, the application may exhibit undesirable effects when
 running under DESQview, but not while running under DOS alone.

 Two TSRs, NODESQ.COM and NOVER.COM, are now included with DESQview.
 NODESQ, when loaded as a TSR in a DESQview window, will respond
 "No" when a program asks "Am I running under DESQview?"; NOVER will
 respond "No" when a program asks "Am I running under TopView?"  If
 you experience a problem with a program in a DESQview window that
 is not reproducible outside of DESQview, it is worth loading NODESQ
 or NOVER in a Big DOS window.  Then try to load the offending
 program in that same window. The program will believe that it is
 not running under DESQview or TopView, and will behave as if it
 were not DESQview-aware.  Typically this will not present a
 problem; as your DESQview manual explains, although DESQview
 awareness is beneficial, a program does not need to be
 DESQview-aware to run well under DESQview.  If the program runs
 well in a window with NODESQ, you may create a batch file which
 loads NODESQ or NOVER and then your program; then use DESQview's
 "Change a Program" to specify the name of this batch file, rather
 than the name of the offending program, on the Program line.

 Several terminal emulation programs and Banyan VINES' Mail program
 will benefit from having NODESQ loaded before them.  Note:
 applications which have a good reason for not running under
 multitasking environments (for example, MS-DOS' CHKDSK) will also
 be fooled by NODESQ or NOVER, so use these TSRs with discretion.


 Logical Disk Drives
 
 Earlier versions of DESQview supported logical drives as an
 internal DESQview option.  DESQview no longer supports this option.


 Hardware and Software Compatibility Notes
 =========================================

 AST PREMIUM EXEC 386SX/20 NOTEBOOK
 ACER 1100SX

 If a mouse is not attached to the computer, you may experience a
 delay of a minute or more when starting DESQview on the AST Premium
 Exec 386SX/20 Notebook or on the Acer 1100SX.  The delay is due to
 timing problems with a BIOS call that DESQview makes to get
 information about port mice.  The delay vanishes if a mouse is
 plugged into the mouse port on these machines; it is reduced to
 8-15 seconds if you add the TRAP8042:Y (T8:Y) parameter to the
 QEMM386.SYS line in CONFIG.SYS (use the IGNOREA20 parameter instead
 with versions of QEMM earlier than 7.0).  There is also a technical
 note called DELAYMS.PAT, #201 on the Quarterdeck Q/FAX service at
 (310) 314- 3214, that will instruct you on how to patch DESQview's
 DV.EXE file to remove the delay.


 PAGEMAKER 4 .DVP FILE

 When installing PageMaker 4 onto your DESQview menu, you may need
 to enter the name of the directory where Windows is installed onto
 the Change a Program screen. This information should be placed in
 the "Directory" field of the Change a Program Standard Options
 screen.


 INTEL SATISFAXTION

 If you wish to use the Intel SatisFAXtion board in conjunction with
 DESQview, obtain the file SATISFAX.ZIP from Quarterdeck Technical
 Support.  This file contains files and documentation detailing
 several options for using the SatisFAXtion board with DESQview.


 BANYAN VINES MAIL

 An apparent bug in Banyan Vines Mail's DESQview API support causes
 screen corruption in various places under DESQview, and in some
 configurations DESQview's Mark feature may not work successfully.

 NODESQ.COM, noted above, solves this problem.  Simply load NODESQ
 in a batch file before loading Banyan Mail, set Virtualize and
 Writes Text both to Y, and Mail and DESQview will work perfectly
 together.


