 Revision History
 ================

 DESQview 2.8, dated 3/15/96, incorporates these changes:

 - DESQview extends support for printing under Novell Netware's VLM
   drivers.

 - The startup sequence for DESQview now distinguishes between
   computers with Micro Channel Architecture buses and and PCI
   machines that include Micro Channel support.

 - DESQview works around a problem with DOS's management of the
   system date at midnight.  If a program other than DOS makes a
   call to determine aspects of the system's date and time, DOS
   will lose track of the number of times that the date has
   changed.  This problem in fact has nothing to do with DESQview,
   but has been observed by DESQview users who leave their systems
   unattended for several days at a time.

 - DESQview can accept a DESQview Program Information File (a file
   with the .DVP extension) as a startup parameter; this will cause
   the specified DVP to be loaded automatically as DESQview starts.
   The user may choose to start other applications, and programs may
   also use the DESQview API to spawn other programs. DESQview v2.42
   would shut down automatically when the last open program was
   exited.  DESQview 2.8 shuts down when the program specified on
   the command line is exited, even when other applications are
   still open.

   The /QX:Y (QuitOnPifExit:Yes) parameter causes DESQview to
   attempt to quit when the program specified on the command line
   exits.  If you use the /QX:N parameter (or simply /QX), DESQview
   not attempt to quit when the program specified on the command
   line exits, and will wait instead until the last process is
   freed.  If no DVP is specified on the command line, then QX is
   ignored.

 DESQview 2.7, dated 5/12/95, incorporated the following changes
 from the previous version of DESQview:

 - The updated DESQview 386 2.7 included QEMM 7.5.

 - Support was added for running DESQview on a peer-to-peer network
   server, and for logging in to Novell networks from within a
   DESQview window; more extensive information on these network
   features may be found above in the section titled "Networks"
   above.

 - This version added support for the playing of audio Compact Discs
   (CDs) from within a DESQview window while multitasking other
   applications.

 - DESQview included a accommodation for the Norton AntiVirus NAVTSR
   program.

 - Version 2.7 added support for users of Novell's SPX (specifically
   WSLAN).

 - Support for the mouse under Novell DOS 7 was improved.

 - A minor problem, wherein WordPerfect would not detect the
   appropriate type of keyboard while running under DESQview, was
   fixed.

 - DESQview 2.7 included support for programs which directly
   manipulate the VGA palette from with a DESQview window.


 Technical Notes
 ===============

 A number of technical notes are available in the DESQview
 directory.  These notes contain valuable information on DESQview,
 and hints and tips for getting more out of the DESQview
 environment.  You may view or print these notes using any file
 viewer, editor, or word processor that can handle plain ASCII text.

 286.TEC: DESQview runs on ANY processor compatible with the Intel
 8086 family.  This note describes the issues involved with running
 DESQview on 80286, 8086, and 8088 processors.

 AUTOSCRI.TEC:  Using DESQview's autostart scripts to open windows,
 position them on the screen, load files within those window, etc.
 -- automatically!

 BANYAN.TEC:  A discussion of Banyan networking software and
 Quarterdeck products.

 CHOOSDIR.TEC:  How to configure programs running under DESQview so
 that you can choose an appropriate directory on startup.

 COM3N4.TEC:  How DESQview handles COM3 and COM4.  Explains why a
 single IRQ can't be shared by multiple COM ports.  Suggests IRQs
 that can be used.  Mentions FOSSIL drivers as a possible solution to
 the "COM port barrier".

 COMMPROG.TEC:  Information on setting up communications programs to
 run in background under DESQview.

 DBASE.TEC:  Information on running dBASE III and dBASE IV in
 DESQview.  Describes a problem with color support, which is a bug
 in dBASE's support of DESQview, and gives suggestions for getting
 around the problem.

 DESQMEM.TEC:  A discussion of how much memory DESQview uses, where
 it uses memory, and where it can load window overhead.

 DESQTEMP.TEC:  How to use a little-known feature of DESQview (the
 DESQTMP?.* filename) to transfer data between programs; the
 advantages of this technique over Mark and Transfer, and how to use
 it to enhance DESQview scripts.

 DEVCHAIN.TEC:  Explanation of the DOS device chain, and how to
 successfully run programs within DESQview that break the chain.

 DIRECTLY.TEC:  This note answers the question: "How can I tell if
 my program writes directly  to the screen?"  It also discusses how
 much memory to give an application running in DESQview.

 DV386.TEC:  An explanation of the differences between DESQview and
 DESQview 386.

 DVANSI.TEC:  How to run programs that require the services of
 ANSI.SYS inside a DESQview window by using the DVANSI.COM program
 that comes with DESQview.

 DVAWARE.TEC:  How to make your standard DOS application
 "DESQview-aware", and notes on why you should do this.

 DVFLOPPY.TEC:  This note explains why floppy drive access can cause
 other applications in DESQview to slow down.

 DVHERC.TEC:  Using the DVHERC.COM program to correct problems with
 running DESQview on Hercules-compatible monochrome graphics cards.

 KBMOUSE.TEC:  What is that diamond that appears on the screen when
 you tap the Control key under DESQview?  Eeek!  It's the keyboard
 mouse!  This note explains what the keyboard mouse does and how to
 disable it if you prefer not to use it.

 KEYBCONF.TEC:  A discussion of the "Keyboard Conflict" field on the
 second page of DESQview's Change a Program menu, and some
 suggestions on how it can help keyboard-related problems under
 DESQview.

 LOGIN.TEC:  A feature in DESQview 2.6 and later greatly improves
 DESQview's support of Novell NetWare, and allows for multiple
 logins under DESQview.

 MAKEADVP.TEC:  Advice for developers and end-users on creating
 DESQview Program Information Files (.DVPs), with emphasis on
 flexibility and portability.

 MAXMEM.TEC:  Providing the maximum possible amount of conventional
 memory in DOS, DESQview, DESQview/X, and Microsoft Windows.

 MEMSIZE.TEC:  A discussion of the Memory Size fields in DESQview's
 Change a Program Menus.

 MEMSTAT.TEC:  An explanation of the information provided by
 DESQview's Memory Status program, and how this information relates
 to your ability to multitask large applications.

 MOUSEDRP.TEC:  What to do if you encounter unsightly "mouse
 droppings" -- colored text blocks that are left in the wake of the
 mouse -- under DESQview.

 PROTECT.TEC:  A discussion of the Protection Level field on the
 second page of DESQview's Change a Program Menu; what the settings
 do and don't do.

 QEXT.TEC:  Documentation for DESQview's XMS driver, QEXT.SYS.

 SHARED.TEC:  DESQview Shared Programs -- how they work, how to
 configure them, and information on writing them.

 SYSTKEYS.TEC:  An explanation of how to change your DESQview system
 keys.

 TAMEBYTE.TEC:  How to improve the performance of your system when a
 program running in background under DESQview or DESQview/X.

 VIRTUALI.TEC:  This note discusses what screen virtualization is,
 why it is needed, and how to use it.  This information is important
 if you wish to run graphical programs in background under DESQview
 386.

 WINSIZE.TEC:  Suggestions on increasing the memory size of your
 windows under DESQview.

